---
const pillars = [
  {
    icon: '⚡',
    title: 'Performance by default',
    body: "Static HTML first, hydration last (if at all). You ship what the page actually needs — not a 200&nbsp;KB runtime to render a marketing headline.",
  },
  {
    icon: '🗻',
    title: 'No SPA tax',
    body: "Marketing pages, docs, blogs — they don't need a virtual DOM. Astro gives you components without the bundle bloat, and Alpine handles the dropdown.",
  },
  {
    icon: '🛰️',
    title: 'The right amount of JS',
    body: "Sprinkle Alpine where interactivity matters. Skip it everywhere else. Your Lighthouse score stops being a guilty conversation at standup.",
  },
];
---

<section id="why" class="relative py-24 sm:py-32">
  <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
    <div class="max-w-2xl">
      <p class="text-sm font-mono text-cosmos-400 uppercase tracking-widest">// Why Alpinaut</p>
      <h2 class="mt-3 text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-bold text-white tracking-tight">
        You shipped a landing page. Why did it download a SPA?
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-slate-300 text-lg">
        Most "modern" stacks treat every page like a complex app. Astro + Alpine treats
        every page like a page — and an app like an app. Here's what shifts when you do that.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-12 grid gap-6 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3">
      {pillars.map((p) => (
        <article class="card rounded-2xl p-6">
          <div class="text-3xl" aria-hidden="true">{p.icon}</div>
          <h3 class="mt-4 text-lg font-semibold text-white">{p.title}</h3>
          <p class="mt-2 text-slate-400 leading-relaxed" set:html={p.body} />
        </article>
      ))}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
